  "report": "The data contain a noisy, one-dimensional closed outline of a sword, revealed by the two modulo-1 combinations in map. The blade points upward near (0.465,0.83), the curved crossguard spans approximately u=0.325 to 0.635 near v=0.50, and the rounded handle ends near (0.49,0.25). These combinations involve columns x2,x3,x4,x9,x12,x14,x15,x20,x25,x27,x28,x29. The outline stays inside the unit square, so there is no wrap-border crossing. The drawing is a 160-vertex closed polyline following the center of the thin noisy band. I used only X.csv, which actually contains 8000 rows and 30 columns. Histograms, correlations, PCA, pairwise contingency tests, nearest-neighbor comparisons, noise classifiers, and small Fourier searches initially looked like independent uniform noise. The decisive search compared products of complex Fourier features from every three-column integer combination with coefficient magnitudes 1 or 2, finding six-column modular relations. On 3000 separate validation rows the two relations had Fourier concentrations 0.89545 and 0.62564, far above the uniform-noise scale of about 0.018. Plotting the two decoded coordinates exposed the sword. I fit the polyline using only the first 5000 decoded rows, using cluster initialization, a closed traveling-salesman ordering, and a regularized principal-curve fit. On the remaining 3000 rows the median Euclidean distance to the drawing was 0.002711; 98.27% were within 0.01. For 100000 independent uniform points, which have exactly the mapped noise distribution because the expressions use disjoint column sets, the median distance was 0.210471 and 3.318% were within 0.01. Distances here are in the raw map coordinates; both noise outputs have standard deviation 1/sqrt(12). Scripts, numerical progress logs, and diagnostic and validation plots are saved in analysis_outputs and the analysis scripts."
}
